<subsection xml:id="subsec-gradient-divergence-curl">
  <title>Gradient Fields, Divergence, and Curl</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Every smooth scalar field gives rise to a vector field via the gradient.
      The reverse question — whether a given vector field comes from some scalar
      field — leads to the concepts of <em>antiderivative</em> and
      <em>conservative field</em>.
      Two further differential operators, <em>divergence</em> and
      <em>curl</em>, capture how a vector field spreads outward from a point
      or how it swirls around an axis.
      These three operations — gradient, divergence, and curl — are the core
      tools of vector calculus.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition: gradient field, antiderivative, potential, conservative ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-gradient-field-potential">
    <title>Gradient Field, Antiderivative, Scalar Potential, Conservative Field</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>D \subseteq \R^n</m> and let <m>f\colon D \to \R</m> be a
        differentiable scalar function.  The <term>gradient field</term> of
        <m>f</m> is the vector field
        <me>
          \nabla f \colon D \to \R^n, \qquad
          \mathbf{x} \mapsto
          \begin{pmatrix}
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \\[4pt]
            \vdots \\[2pt]
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_n}(\mathbf{x})
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{F}\colon D \to \R^n</m> be a vector field.
        A differentiable scalar function <m>f\colon D \to \R</m> satisfying
        <m>\nabla f = \mathbf{F}</m> is called an <term>antiderivative</term>
        (or <term>potential function</term>) of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In force-field applications one often uses the negative function
        <m>U = -f</m> as a potential energy, so that
        <m>\mathbf{F} = -\nabla U</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A vector field that possesses an antiderivative is called a
        <term>conservative vector field</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Example: central force field has antiderivative ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-central-force-antiderivative">
    <title>The Gravitational Field Is Conservative</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the central force field
        <m>\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}) = -\dfrac{MG}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^3}\,\mathbf{x}</m>
        on <m>\R^3 \setminus \{\mathbf{0}\}</m> has the antiderivative
        <m>f(\mathbf{x}) = \dfrac{MG}{\|\mathbf{x}\|}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We compute <m>\nabla f</m> and check that it equals
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m>.  Write
        <m>\|\mathbf{x}\| = \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + x_3^2}</m>.
        Differentiating with respect to <m>x_1</m>:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \frac{\partial f}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x})
            \amp= \frac{\partial}{\partial x_1}
                 \bigl(MG\,(x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2)^{-1/2}\bigr)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= MG \cdot \underbrace{2x_1}_{\text{inner deriv.}} \cdot
                 \Bigl(-\tfrac{1}{2}\Bigr)
                 (x_1^2+x_2^2+x_3^2)^{-3/2}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= -\frac{MG\,x_1}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^3}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
        By symmetry the same calculation applies to <m>x_2</m> and
        <m>x_3</m>, giving
        <me>
          \nabla f(\mathbf{x})
          = -\frac{MG}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^3}
            \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix}
          = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}).
        </me>
        Hence <m>f</m> is an antiderivative of <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, and the
        gravitational field is conservative.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Definition: divergence and curl ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-divergence-curl">
    <title>Divergence and Curl</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>D \subseteq \R^n</m> and let
        <m>\mathbf{v}\colon D \to \R^n</m> be a differentiable vector field.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <term>divergence</term> of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is the scalar field
        <me>
          \operatorname{div}\mathbf{v} \colon D \to \R, \qquad
          \mathbf{x} \mapsto
          \frac{\partial v_1}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x})
          + \frac{\partial v_2}{\partial x_2}(\mathbf{x})
          + \cdots
          + \frac{\partial v_n}{\partial x_n}(\mathbf{x}).
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>n = 3</m>, the <term>curl</term> of <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        the vector field
        <me>
          \operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v} \colon D \to \R^3, \qquad
          \mathbf{x} \mapsto
          \begin{pmatrix}
            \dfrac{\partial v_3}{\partial x_2}(\mathbf{x})
            - \dfrac{\partial v_2}{\partial x_3}(\mathbf{x}) \\[6pt]
            \dfrac{\partial v_1}{\partial x_3}(\mathbf{x})
            - \dfrac{\partial v_3}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x}) \\[6pt]
            \dfrac{\partial v_2}{\partial x_1}(\mathbf{x})
            - \dfrac{\partial v_1}{\partial x_2}(\mathbf{x})
          \end{pmatrix}.
        </me>
        It is also written <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Physical interpretation.</em>
        The divergence measures the <em>net outward flux per unit volume</em>
        at a point: a positive value indicates a source (fluid spreads outward),
        a negative value indicates a sink.
        The curl measures the <em>circulation density</em>: its magnitude
        gives the local rotation rate and its direction gives the axis of
        rotation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Remark: field operations diagram ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-field-operations-diagram">
    <title>Map of Field Operations</title>
    <p>
      The gradient, divergence, and curl, together with the
      <term>Laplacian</term>
      <m>\Delta f = \operatorname{div}(\nabla f)
         = \dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_1^2} + \cdots
         + \dfrac{\partial^2 f}{\partial x_n^2}</m>,
      form a coherent system of differential operators linking scalar and
      vector fields:
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-field-operations-diagram">
      <caption>
        Differential operators connecting scalar and vector fields.
        The gravitational pair <m>f(\mathbf{x}) = MG/\|\mathbf{x}\|</m>
        and <m>\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}) = -MG\,\mathbf{x}/\|\mathbf{x}\|^3</m>
        is shown as a concrete instance.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-field-operations-diagram" width="70%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[
            node distance=2.4cm and 3.6cm,
            box/.style={draw,rounded corners,minimum width=2.8cm,
                        minimum height=1.0cm,align=center,font=\small},
            arr/.style={->,thick,shorten >=2pt,shorten &lt;=2pt}
          ]
            \node[box] (sf) {Scalar field\\$f: D \to \R$};
            \node[box, right=of sf] (vf) {Vector field\\$\mathbf{v}: D \to \R^n$};
            % grad: scalar -> vector
            \draw[arr,bend left=18] (sf) to
              node[above,font=\small] {$\nabla$ (gradient)} (vf);
            % div: vector -> scalar
            \draw[arr,bend left=18] (vf) to
              node[below,font=\small] {$\operatorname{div}$ (divergence)} (sf);
            % rot: vector -> vector (loop on vf)
            \draw[arr] (vf.north east)
              to[out=60,in=30,looseness=3]
              node[right,font=\small] {$\operatorname{rot}$ (curl)} (vf.south east);
            % Laplacian: scalar -> scalar (loop on sf)
            \draw[arr] (sf.north west)
              to[out=150,in=120,looseness=3]
              node[left,font=\small] {$\Delta = \operatorname{div}(\nabla\,\cdot)$} (sf.south west);
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Example: computing divergence and curl ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-compute-divergence-curl">
    <title>Computing Divergence and Curl</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y,z)
          = \begin{pmatrix} x^2 \\ yz \\ \sin y \end{pmatrix}</m>.
        Compute <m>\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}</m> and
        <m>\operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Divergence:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}
            \amp= \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2)
               + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(yz)
               + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(\sin y)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp= 2x + z + 0 = 2x + z.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Curl:</em>
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v}
            \amp=
            \begin{pmatrix}
              \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(\sin y)
              - \dfrac{\partial}{\partial z}(yz) \\[6pt]
              \dfrac{\partial}{\partial z}(x^2)
              - \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(\sin y) \\[6pt]
              \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x}(yz)
              - \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}(x^2)
            \end{pmatrix}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp=
            \begin{pmatrix}
              \cos y - y \\
              0 - 0 \\
              0 - 0
            \end{pmatrix}
            =
            \begin{pmatrix} \cos y - y \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-gradient-divergence-curl-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-gradient-field-conservative">
      <title>Gradient Fields</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If a vector field can be written as <m>\mathbf{v}=\nabla f</m> for some scalar function <m>f</m>, what is it called?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>A conservative vector field</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. A gradient field is also called conservative and has a scalar potential.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>A rotationally symmetric field</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Gradient fields need not be rotationally symmetric.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>A divergence-free field</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. A gradient field need not have zero divergence.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>A constant field</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Gradient fields can vary from point to point.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-divergence-curl-simple">
      <title>Compute Divergence and Curl</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}(x,y,z)=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix},
          </me>
          compute <m>\operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>\operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The divergence is
          <me>
            \operatorname{div}\mathbf{v}
            = \frac{\partial x}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial y}{\partial y}+\frac{\partial z}{\partial z}
            = 1+1+1=3.
          </me>
          The curl is
          <me>
            \operatorname{rot}\mathbf{v}
            =
            \begin{pmatrix}
              \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}-\frac{\partial y}{\partial z}\\
              \frac{\partial x}{\partial z}-\frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\\
              \frac{\partial y}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}
            \end{pmatrix}
            =
            \begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\0\end{pmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
